sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-23> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Computer experiments should study the simplest possible systems with the simplest possible procedures, because simple systems already show complex behavior, and the most common mistake is studying systems far more complicated than necessary.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "One can never start a computer experiment with too simple a system";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “How the Discoveries in This Chapter Were Made” (from p. 108), p. 110. Scope: design of computer experiments"
}
